messages:
jordan: Hey team — getting 401 invalid_api_key from our Cloudflare Worker. Key shows active in dashboard and the same key works from my laptop, but all requests from the worker return 401. Any hints?

liam: Hi Jordan — can you paste a minimal repro (curl or worker snippet) and whether this is hosted or dedicated? Also include a redacted key if possible.

jordan: Hosted. Minimal curl we used from our worker (we obfuscated the key in logs):
```
curl -i -H "Authorization: Bearer sk_live_********" https://api.redwood.ai/v1/responses
HTTP/1.1 401 Unauthorized
{"error":"invalid_api_key","message":"API key not found"}
```

liam: Thanks. Two quick checks: 1) any middleware that encodes/modifies the Authorization header? 2) is the Worker adding any extra headers (X-*) or stripping spaces?

jordan: We do a little telemetry encoding in the worker. Relevant snippet:
```js
addEventListener('fetch', event => {
  event.respondWith(handle(event.request))
})
async function handle(req) {
  const auth = req.headers.get('authorization')
  // we base64 the auth token for our logs/telemetry
  const encoded = btoa(auth)
  return fetch('https://api.redwood.ai/v1/responses', {
    method: 'POST',
    headers: { 'authorization': encoded, 'content-type': 'application/json' },
    body: req.body
  })
}
```

sophia: That would definitely break it — Redwood expects the raw Bearer header. What does the logged header look like on the failing requests?

jordan: Logged header sample (obfuscated): "Authorization: YmVhcmVyIHNrX2xpdmVfZW5jb2RlZA=="

liam: YmVhcmVyIHNrX2xpdmVfZW5jb2RlZA== base64-decodes to "Bearer sk_live_encoded" — so the worker is sending an encoded value instead of the literal header. That explains why the hosted curl from your laptop worked (it bypasses the telemetry path) and the worker fails.

oncall_sre: One more twist — we recently enabled client-bound keys for some orgs. If the API key is bound to a client tag, requests must include X-Client-Tag header matching the allowed tag, otherwise we reject with 401 too. The error text can look the same as invalid_api_key in some cases. I checked the key metadata for sk_live_abc... and it has allowed_client_tags: ["cf-worker-app"].

liam: So two possible causes here: encoded Authorization header, and missing X-Client-Tag. Quick remediation steps: 1) send the literal Bearer header (remove base64) or decode before sending, 2) add header `X-Client-Tag: cf-worker-app` in the worker request, or 3) we can temporarily remove the binding on the key if you want immediate access while you patch the worker.

jordan: Implemented fix: decoded the header and added `X-Client-Tag: cf-worker-app` in the worker. After deploy I see 200 OK. Thanks everyone! :tada:

support-bot: Ticket #INC-2026-154 created and attached to the thread for follow-up (owner: liam).

liam: Great — leaving this open for a day in case the customer wants us to remove binding or rotate the key. Jordan, ping here if anything reoccurs. :thumbsup:
